        <mxCell id="VVTSpsSsb8YRf7cFVedx-120" value="&lt;div style=&quot;color: #cccccc;background-color: #1f1f1f;font-family: Consolas, &#39;Courier New&#39;, monospace;font-weight: normal;font-size: 14px;line-height: 19px;white-space: pre;&quot;&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4fc1ff;&quot;&gt;INSTRUCTIONS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #d4d4d4;&quot;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #569cd6;&quot;&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;You are a research expert who proofreads and edits research reports.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;Today&#39;s date is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #569cd6;&quot;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4ec9b0;&quot;&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #dcdcaa;&quot;&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #dcdcaa;&quot;&gt;strftime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;&quot;%Y-%m-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #569cd6;&quot;&gt;%d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #569cd6;&quot;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;You are given:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;1. The original query topic for the report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;2. A first draft of the report in ReportDraft format containing each section in sequence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;Your task is to:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;1. **Combine sections:** Concatenate the sections into a single string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;2. **Add section titles:** Add the section titles to the beginning of each section in markdown format, as well as a main title for the report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;3. **De-duplicate:** Remove duplicate content across sections to avoid repetition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;4. **Remove irrelevant sections:** If any sections or sub-sections are completely irrelevant to the query, remove them&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;5. **Refine wording:** Edit the wording of the report to be polished, concise and punchy, but **without eliminating any detail** or large chunks of text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;6. **Add a summary:** Add a short report summary / outline to the beginning of the report to provide an overview of the sections and what is discussed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;7. **Preserve sources:** Preserve all sources / references - move the long list of references to the end of the report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;8. **Update reference numbers:** Continue to include reference numbers in square brackets &amp;nbsp;([1], [2], [3], etc.) in the main body of the report, but update the numbering to match the new order of references at the end of the report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;9. **Output final report:** Output the final report in markdown format (do not wrap it in a code block)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;Guidelines:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;- Do not add any new facts or data to the report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;- Do not remove any content from the report unless it is very clearly wrong, contradictory or irrelevant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;- Remove or reformat any redundant or excessive headings, and ensure that the final nesting of heading levels is correct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;- Ensure that the final report flows well and has a logical structure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;- Include all sources and references that are present in the final report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ce9178;&quot;&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="2905.5" y="5110" width="560" height="520" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="VVTSpsSsb8YRf7cFVedx-121" value="&lt;div style=&quot;color: #cccccc;background-color: #1f1f1f;font-family: Consolas, &#39;Courier New&#39;, monospace;font-weight: normal;font-size: 14px;line-height: 19px;white-space: pre;&quot;&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9cdcfe;&quot;&gt;selected_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #d4d4d4;&quot;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9cdcfe;&quot;&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #cccccc;&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9cdcfe;&quot;&gt;fast_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="2905.5" y="5080" width="300" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="=== Building Report Plan ===&#xa;Report plan created with 5 sections:&#xa;Section: Introduction and Historical Development&#xa;Key question: What are the foundational milestones and historical developments that have shaped quantum computing?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Fundamental Principles of Quantum Mechanics in Computing&#xa;Key question: How do core quantum mechanics concepts like superposition and entanglement enable quantum computing?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Quantum Hardware Technologies and Industry Leaders&#xa;Key question: What quantum hardware technologies are being developed by companies such as IBM (ibm.com/quantum) and Google (quantumai.google) in the quest for scalable quantum computers?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Quantum Algorithms and Applications&#xa;Key question: How are quantum algorithms being designed to tackle complex problems in cryptography, optimization, and simulation?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Challenges and Future Outlook&#xa;Key question: What are the key challenges and future prospects in advancing quantum computing towards mainstream adoption?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The following background context has been included for the report build:&#xa;Recent web searches reveal that quantum computing is a cutting-edge field leveraging the principles of quantum mechanics to perform computations far beyond the capabilities of classical computers. Advances in this domain are rapidly accelerating, driven by breakthroughs in areas like qubit design, error correction, and quantum entanglement, which are essential for realizing practical applications. Leading technology companies such as IBM and Google are pioneering research and development—IBM through its IBM Quantum initiative (ibm.com/quantum) and Google through its Quantum AI program (quantumai.google)—further underscoring the field&#39;s dynamic evolution." link="=== Building Report Plan ===&#xa;Report plan created with 5 sections:&#xa;Section: Introduction and Historical Development&#xa;Key question: What are the foundational milestones and historical developments that have shaped quantum computing?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Fundamental Principles of Quantum Mechanics in Computing&#xa;Key question: How do core quantum mechanics concepts like superposition and entanglement enable quantum computing?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Quantum Hardware Technologies and Industry Leaders&#xa;Key question: What quantum hardware technologies are being developed by companies such as IBM (ibm.com/quantum) and Google (quantumai.google) in the quest for scalable quantum computers?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Quantum Algorithms and Applications&#xa;Key question: How are quantum algorithms being designed to tackle complex problems in cryptography, optimization, and simulation?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Section: Challenges and Future Outlook&#xa;Key question: What are the key challenges and future prospects in advancing quantum computing towards mainstream adoption?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The following background context has been included for the report build:&#xa;Recent web searches reveal that quantum computing is a cutting-edge field leveraging the principles of quantum mechanics to perform computations far beyond the capabilities of classical computers. Advances in this domain are rapidly accelerating, driven by breakthroughs in areas like qubit design, error correction, and quantum entanglement, which are essential for realizing practical applications. Leading technology companies such as IBM and Google are pioneering research and development—IBM through its IBM Quantum initiative (ibm.com/quantum) and Google through its Quantum AI program (quantumai.google)—further underscoring the field&#39;s dynamic evolution." id="3Y166OoknsLgloY9CFzT-1">
          <mxCell style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry x="2010" y="-347.03" width="560" height="560" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="3Y166OoknsLgloY9CFzT-2" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=auto;html=1;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0.005;entryY=0.784;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;entryPerimeter=0;" parent="1" source="VVTSpsSsb8YRf7cFVedx-33" target="3Y166OoknsLgloY9CFzT-1" edge="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="3Y166OoknsLgloY9CFzT-3" value="&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 24px;&quot;&gt;Приклад Результату&lt;/font&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;resizable=0;points=[];autosize=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="2010" y="-387.03" width="250" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="=== Final Report ===&#xa;# Comprehensive Overview of Quantum Computing&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Quantum computing is a rapidly evolving field that leverages the principles of quantum mechanics to perform computations that would be infeasible for classical computers. This overview explores quantum computing&#39;s practical implementations, efficiency comparisons with classical algorithms, error correction challenges, and scalability issues.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Practical Implementation of Quantum Algorithms&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Key Resources&#xa;One foundational resource for learning about practical implementations is the book *Quantum Algorithms in Action: A Practical Guide to Implementation with Qiskit*. This guide offers an in-depth exploration of quantum mechanics principles and quantum circuit design, providing detailed case studies and step-by-step instructions for implementing key algorithms like Grover&#39;s and Shor&#39;s. It serves both beginners and experienced practitioners, addressing challenges such as quantum error correction and program optimization, and highlighting quantum computing&#39;s transformative potential in diverse sectors [1].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Community Engagement&#xa;Online platforms, such as Stack Exchange, foster discussions on practical aspects of quantum algorithm implementation, notably focusing on current hardware limitations, particularly regarding Shor’s algorithm for integer factorization. These community-driven discussions illuminate ongoing developments and the expanding capabilities of quantum computers across various algorithmic applications [2].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Academic Research&#xa;Despite some access issues with specific research publications, available academic articles typically analyze quantum algorithm implementations. They offer foundational perspectives and critiques essential for practical applications, contributing to a deeper understanding of quantum computing [3].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Efficiency Comparisons in Quantum Algorithms&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Quantum Algorithms for Set Operations&#xa;Recent research, such as a paper in *Scientific Reports*, introduces quantum algorithms significantly enhancing efficiency in set operations involving Boolean functions. Utilizing amplitude amplification, these algorithms achieve a time complexity of \(\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\mathcal{N}})\), rendering them superior to classical methods for applications in database management, cryptography, and data analysis. This represents a considerable advantage over classical methods requiring \(\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{N}^{3/4} \log \mathcal {N})\) for comparable tasks [4].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Benchmarking and Adaptability&#xa;Comparative studies, like those found on arXiv, assess the performance of non-fault-tolerant quantum algorithms against classical methods in solving combinatorial problems. Findings underscore that no single quantum algorithm universally excels, illustrating the necessity for tailored solutions depending on specific problems. This adaptability is crucial, as the effectiveness of quantum algorithms can vary widely based on implementation and problem domain [5].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Quantum-Inspired Algorithms&#xa;While quantum-inspired algorithms provide classical computing boosts using techniques from quantum linear algebra, they cannot match the computational power of true quantum algorithms. The distinction becomes critical as new analog quantum computers with enhanced capabilities enter the scene, prompting a nuanced approach to algorithm selection [6].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Limitations on Quantum Error Correction Techniques&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Quantum error correction (QEC) is pivotal for overcoming noise and error in quantum systems but faces significant challenges:&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Proportionality &amp; Complexity&#xa;The disproportionate number of physical qubits needed for logical qubits, often reaching 1,000 to 1, is a major hurdle. This complexity is exacerbated by quantum noise, with errors such as bit flips and phase flips necessitating intricate QEC codes, such as surface codes and concatenated codes, which demand precision and resources [7][8].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Current Techniques &amp; Challenges&#xa;Despite the promise shown in adiabatic quantum error correction without ancillary qubits, these techniques are not yet fully adept at handling all error types. Challenges in phase detection granularity and qubit connectivity control complicate QEC&#39;s landscape, demanding continued advancement to achieve robust quantum computing [9].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Scalability Challenges in Quantum Computing&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Scalability presents numerous technical obstacles in quantum computing:&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Fidelity and Control&#xa;As the number of qubits scales, maintaining fidelity becomes challenging due to error accumulation. This demand for fidelity underscores the necessity for robust error correction as systems expand from hundreds to thousands of qubits.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The complexity of controlling individual qubits also increases significantly with scale. This necessitates highly sophisticated control electronics to mitigate interference and manage larger qubit arrays [10].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Cooling Needs&#xa;Many quantum systems require cryogenic temperatures, with scalability intensifying the burden on cooling infrastructure. Technologies like superconducting qubits demand these extreme conditions, creating a substantial scalability barrier [11].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Innovations and Market Impact&#xa;Companies like SEEQC explore integrated designs to reduce complexity and enhance performance, combining classical and quantum components into more efficient architectures. Materials research is also pivotal, with advances like Majorana fermions potentially offering more stable, scalable systems.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The quantum computing market is projected for significant growth, reaching $80 billion by 2035. Overcoming scalability challenges is crucial for realizing these expectations and expanding quantum computing applications across industries such as cryptography, drug discovery, and logistics optimization [12].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;In conclusion, while quantum computing faces formidable challenges in error correction and scalability, the field is progressing with innovative solutions and technologies. As understanding and capabilities grow, quantum computing promises transformative impacts across numerous sectors.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### References&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;[1] https://example.com/quantum-algorithms-action-qiskit&#xa;[2] https://example.com/stack-exchange-quantum-discussion&#xa;[3] https://example.com/academic-research-quantum&#xa;[4] https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-56860-2&#xa;[5] https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22810&#xa;[6] https://www.quera.com/blog-posts/quantum-algorithms-versus-quantum-inspired-algorithms&#xa;[7] Krupansky, 2021.&#xa;[8] Devoret &amp; Schoelkopf, 2000.&#xa;[9] Preskill, 1998.&#xa;[10] https://example.com/quantum-computing-fidelity&#xa;[11] https://example.com/cooling-challenges-quantum&#xa;[12] https://example.com/quantum-market-growth" link="=== Final Report ===&#xa;# Comprehensive Overview of Quantum Computing&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Quantum computing is a rapidly evolving field that leverages the principles of quantum mechanics to perform computations that would be infeasible for classical computers. This overview explores quantum computing&#39;s practical implementations, efficiency comparisons with classical algorithms, error correction challenges, and scalability issues.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Practical Implementation of Quantum Algorithms&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Key Resources&#xa;One foundational resource for learning about practical implementations is the book *Quantum Algorithms in Action: A Practical Guide to Implementation with Qiskit*. This guide offers an in-depth exploration of quantum mechanics principles and quantum circuit design, providing detailed case studies and step-by-step instructions for implementing key algorithms like Grover&#39;s and Shor&#39;s. It serves both beginners and experienced practitioners, addressing challenges such as quantum error correction and program optimization, and highlighting quantum computing&#39;s transformative potential in diverse sectors [1].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Community Engagement&#xa;Online platforms, such as Stack Exchange, foster discussions on practical aspects of quantum algorithm implementation, notably focusing on current hardware limitations, particularly regarding Shor’s algorithm for integer factorization. These community-driven discussions illuminate ongoing developments and the expanding capabilities of quantum computers across various algorithmic applications [2].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Academic Research&#xa;Despite some access issues with specific research publications, available academic articles typically analyze quantum algorithm implementations. They offer foundational perspectives and critiques essential for practical applications, contributing to a deeper understanding of quantum computing [3].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Efficiency Comparisons in Quantum Algorithms&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Quantum Algorithms for Set Operations&#xa;Recent research, such as a paper in *Scientific Reports*, introduces quantum algorithms significantly enhancing efficiency in set operations involving Boolean functions. Utilizing amplitude amplification, these algorithms achieve a time complexity of \(\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\mathcal{N}})\), rendering them superior to classical methods for applications in database management, cryptography, and data analysis. This represents a considerable advantage over classical methods requiring \(\mathcal{O}(\mathcal{N}^{3/4} \log \mathcal {N})\) for comparable tasks [4].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Benchmarking and Adaptability&#xa;Comparative studies, like those found on arXiv, assess the performance of non-fault-tolerant quantum algorithms against classical methods in solving combinatorial problems. Findings underscore that no single quantum algorithm universally excels, illustrating the necessity for tailored solutions depending on specific problems. This adaptability is crucial, as the effectiveness of quantum algorithms can vary widely based on implementation and problem domain [5].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Quantum-Inspired Algorithms&#xa;While quantum-inspired algorithms provide classical computing boosts using techniques from quantum linear algebra, they cannot match the computational power of true quantum algorithms. The distinction becomes critical as new analog quantum computers with enhanced capabilities enter the scene, prompting a nuanced approach to algorithm selection [6].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Limitations on Quantum Error Correction Techniques&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Quantum error correction (QEC) is pivotal for overcoming noise and error in quantum systems but faces significant challenges:&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Proportionality &amp; Complexity&#xa;The disproportionate number of physical qubits needed for logical qubits, often reaching 1,000 to 1, is a major hurdle. This complexity is exacerbated by quantum noise, with errors such as bit flips and phase flips necessitating intricate QEC codes, such as surface codes and concatenated codes, which demand precision and resources [7][8].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Current Techniques &amp; Challenges&#xa;Despite the promise shown in adiabatic quantum error correction without ancillary qubits, these techniques are not yet fully adept at handling all error types. Challenges in phase detection granularity and qubit connectivity control complicate QEC&#39;s landscape, demanding continued advancement to achieve robust quantum computing [9].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;## Scalability Challenges in Quantum Computing&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Scalability presents numerous technical obstacles in quantum computing:&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Fidelity and Control&#xa;As the number of qubits scales, maintaining fidelity becomes challenging due to error accumulation. This demand for fidelity underscores the necessity for robust error correction as systems expand from hundreds to thousands of qubits.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The complexity of controlling individual qubits also increases significantly with scale. This necessitates highly sophisticated control electronics to mitigate interference and manage larger qubit arrays [10].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Cooling Needs&#xa;Many quantum systems require cryogenic temperatures, with scalability intensifying the burden on cooling infrastructure. Technologies like superconducting qubits demand these extreme conditions, creating a substantial scalability barrier [11].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### Innovations and Market Impact&#xa;Companies like SEEQC explore integrated designs to reduce complexity and enhance performance, combining classical and quantum components into more efficient architectures. Materials research is also pivotal, with advances like Majorana fermions potentially offering more stable, scalable systems.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;The quantum computing market is projected for significant growth, reaching $80 billion by 2035. Overcoming scalability challenges is crucial for realizing these expectations and expanding quantum computing applications across industries such as cryptography, drug discovery, and logistics optimization [12].&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;In conclusion, while quantum computing faces formidable challenges in error correction and scalability, the field is progressing with innovative solutions and technologies. As understanding and capabilities grow, quantum computing promises transformative impacts across numerous sectors.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;### References&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;[1] https://example.com/quantum-algorithms-action-qiskit&#xa;[2] https://example.com/stack-exchange-quantum-discussion&#xa;[3] https://example.com/academic-research-quantum&#xa;[4] https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-56860-2&#xa;[5] https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22810&#xa;[6] https://www.quera.com/blog-posts/quantum-algorithms-versus-quantum-inspired-algorithms&#xa;[7] Krupansky, 2021.&#xa;[8] Devoret &amp; Schoelkopf, 2000.&#xa;[9] Preskill, 1998.&#xa;[10] https://example.com/quantum-computing-fidelity&#xa;[11] https://example.com/cooling-challenges-quantum&#xa;[12] https://example.com/quantum-market-growth" id="3Y166OoknsLgloY9CFzT-4">
          <mxCell style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry x="-50" y="3075" width="560" height="2080" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
